# Oasis Tasks - Deep Work Manager

A functional, high-performance task management dashboard built with Vanilla JavaScript, HTML, and CSS. This project focuses on industrial aesthetic design and real-time DOM manipulation to help users eliminate distractions and maintain deep focus.

## 🚀 Key Features
* **Dynamic Task Management**: Add new tasks to your cognitive flow instantly through the interface.
* **Interactive Task Completion**: Toggle tasks as "completed" with a single click, providing immediate visual feedback.
* **Live Progress Tracking**: A real-time progress bar that dynamically calculates your daily performance based on task completion.
* **Rugged & Professional UI**: A clean, triple-column layout inspired by modern productivity workflows.

## 🛠️ Technical Concepts
* **DOM Manipulation**: Implements `document.createElement` and `appendChild` to update the task list without refreshing the page.
* **Event Delegation**: Uses a single event listener on the parent container to handle clicks on dynamically generated tasks efficiently.
* **State Management**: Updates multiple UI components (counters and progress bars) simultaneously whenever the application state changes.

## 📁 Project Structure
* `index.html`: The structural foundation of the dashboard.
* `style.css`: The styling logic for the rugged and industrial aesthetic.
* `script.js`: The functional "brain" of the app, handling all user interactions.
* `/prototypes`: Includes the Lo-Fi wireframes and Hi-Fi designs that guided development.

## 📖 How to Run
1.  Clone the repository to your local machine:
    ```bash
    git clone [https://github.com/nejj0002/final-js-task-manager.git](https://github.com/nejj0002/final-js-task-manager.git)
    ```
2.  Open `index.html` in any modern web browser.
3.  (Recommended) Use the **VS Code Live Server** extension for real-time development previews.

## 🎓 Academic Context
This project was developed for the Final Project of the Interactive Media program. It demonstrates advanced proficiency in Vanilla JavaScript, responsive CSS layouts, and user-centric design.